/** Standalone batch AJ -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output,
 * EXCEPT uva-1730 whose official Sample was corrupted (see below).
 *
 * uva-1657-game: classic "sum and product" mutual-knowledge puzzle -- iteratively filter the
 *   candidate pair set for M rounds (S's turns use sum-grouping, P's turns use product-grouping,
 *   alternating; a pair survives a round iff its group has size > 1, i.e. genuinely ambiguous /
 *   "don't know"), then the (M+1)-th player's statement is "I know", so the answer set is exactly
 *   the surviving candidates whose group (by whichever value that player was told) has size exactly
 *   1. Verified against all 29 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-1660-cable-tv-network: global vertex connectivity -- 0 if disconnected, n if complete (per
 *   the problem's own special rule 1, not the usual n-1 convention), otherwise the minimum over all
 *   non-adjacent vertex pairs of a split-vertex (in/out, capacity 1) max-flow min-cut. Verified
 *   against all 20 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-1714-keyboarding: precompute each cell's 4 directional destinations (the next differently-
 *   keyed cell, or itself if none exists), then process the text character by character with a
 *   Dijkstra-style relaxation carrying forward the TRUE accumulated stroke count per cell (not
 *   resetting to 0 per key) -- this is required for correctness, since collapsing to independent
 *   pairwise key-to-key shortest distances would implicitly assume the same "best" cell within a key
 *   can simultaneously serve as both its optimal entry and exit point, which isn't always true.
 *   Verified against the problem statement's own Sample Input 1 (CONTEST -> 30 strokes) and all 14
 *   other datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-1730-sum-of-mslcm: MSLCM(n) equals sigma(n) (the sum of ALL of n's divisors) -- since every
 *   set achieving LCM = n must consist entirely of divisors of n (any element must divide the LCM),
 *   and the FULL divisor set of n trivially achieves LCM = n while maximizing the sum by definition.
 *   The running total sum_{i=2}^{N} sigma(i) is then computed via the standard O(sqrt(N)) divisor-
 *   block technique (sum_{d=1}^{N} d*floor(N/d), grouping equal floor(N/d) values), using BigInt
 *   throughout for safety at the N<=2*10^7 scale. THE SCRAPED OFFICIAL SAMPLE OUTPUT IS DUPLICATED
 *   (the same 19 answers appear twice for a single copy of the 19-line input) -- a scraper artifact,
 *   not real data; confirmed correct by hand-verifying the formula reproduces the real sample's own
 *   19 values exactly. sampleLimit: 0.
 * uva-1746-string-theory: model the nesting as a nested-nesting stack whose state reduces to just
 *   (currentLevel, hasClosedAtLeastOneChild) since child level is always exactly parent level - 1 (no
 *   gaps possible in the stack); process each quote-run as a per-run reachable-state DP (a run's
 *   quotes may cascade through several opens/closes with no separating text), then search candidate
 *   overall levels K from the theoretical max (bounded by K*(K+1) <= total quote count) downward for
 *   the first K whose full run sequence can reach the fully-closed "DONE" state. A bug caught here:
 *   initially only allowed opening a level's FIRST child when hasChild was false, which incorrectly
 *   rejected valid ADDITIONAL sibling children after the first -- caught by hand-tracing the real
 *   sample's own first worked example (2 1 1 1 3 -> level 2), which requires exactly one such extra
 *   sibling. Verified against all 15 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-11995-i-can-guess-the-data-structure: simulate all three candidate structures (stack, queue,
 *   max-priority-queue) in parallel against the operation log, eliminating any that ever contradicts
 *   an observed pop value; "impossible" if none survive, "not sure" if more than one does. Verified
 *   against all 15 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-12015-google-is-feeling-lucky: collect all pages tied at the maximum relevance value, printed
 *   in original input order. Verified against both datasets in the real sample.
 */
